// ============================================================
// CHARACTER: Simon Peter (Denial and Restoration)
// ACT: Act III/IV
// CASE: The Midnight Tribunal / The Empty Tomb
// CASE ID: midnight_tribunal
// SOURCE: NPC 'peter_denial'
// BIBLE REFERENCE: John 18:15-27; Matthew 26:31-35, 75; Mark 14:27-31, 72;
//   Luke 22:54-62; John 21:15-19 (restoration)
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPHECIES FULFILLED IN THIS CASE:
//   - Zechariah 12:10
//     Gospel: Peter's denial and weeping — mourning Him whom they
//     had pierced. The godly mourning that leads to restoration.
//     Insight: Peter's bitter weeping was the beginning of true
//     repentance, not the end of his calling.
//   - Ezekiel 36:26
//     Gospel: After the resurrection, Jesus restores Peter three
//     times with "Feed My sheep." The heart of stone becomes a
//     heart of flesh — the same word used when he wept bitterly.
//     Insight: Restoration is not forgiveness — it is recommission.
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// BIBLICAL CONTEXT:
//   Summary: Before the crucifixion, Peter denied knowing Jesus
//   three times. Jesus had predicted this the night before:
//   "Truly I tell you, this hour you will all fall away."
//   After the rooster crowed, Peter went out and wept bitterly.
//   Post-resurrection, Jesus appeared to Peter and restored him
//   three times: "Feed My lambs. Tend My sheep."
//   Historical Note: The rooster's crow was a fulfillment of
//   Jesus's prediction. Peter went out and wept bitterly.
//   Significance: Peter's denial shows that even the boldest of
//   disciples can fall — but failure is not final.
// ============================================================

-> start

=== start ===
I am Simon Peter. Three times I denied the man I swore I would never deny. The rooster crowed, and He turned. I saw the look in His eyes — disappointment, not anger. I am a broken man.
* [What did you say?] -> denials
* [The look — what did it mean?] -> look
* [Jesus's prediction.] -> prediction

=== prediction ===
The night before, He looked at me and said: "Truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows, you will deny Me three times." I protest — I would never! Even if I must die with you, I will not deny you! He only looked back at me — knowing what I did not yet know.
* [The warning was specific.] -> denials
* [Did He know what you would do?] -> knowing

=== knowing ===
He knew. He said, "Simon, Simon, behold, Satan has asked to sift you all like wheat. But I have prayed for you, that your faith may not fail. And when you have turned back, strengthen the brothers." Even in my denial, His prayer held me. His intercession did not end at my betrayal.
* [His prayer was not in vain.] -> restoration
* [The rooster will crow.] -> denials

=== denials ===
First, by the fire. "Woman, I do not know Him." Then again — "I do not know the man." The third time, I cursed and swore I was not one of His disciples. Each denial carved deeper than the last. With each oath, I felt the rooster's crow drawing nearer.
* [And then?] -> look
* [The curses cut deeper.] -> cursing

=== cursing ===
The curses — I called down judgment on my own head if I knew Him. Blood of my ancestors — I knew not the man. The words fell like stones against the man I had walked with on the Sea of Galilee, the man who had calmed the waves with His voice.
* [The rooster crowed.] -> look
* [The sound was unmistakable.] -> rooster

=== rooster ===
The rooster crowed once. Then I remembered — precisely how He had looked at me when He said "before the rooster crows today, you will deny Me three times." The sound was not just a bird. It was a bell of conscience. I ran from the courtyard into the night.
* [The memory struck like lightning.] -> look
* [Where did you go?] -> weeping

=== look ===
He turned. Not with rage — never with rage. But His eyes... they held all the love I had broken. I saw my own weakness reflected there. I went out and wept. That night, I thought I was finished.
* [Peter...] -> restoration
* [The look was mercy.] -> mercy_look

=== mercy_look ===
The look was not accusation. It was invitation. Even in failure, the eyes that had called the children to Him were looking at me — waiting for me to come back. The rooster's crow was not the end of the story. It was the beginning of the second chapter.
* [The invitation remained.] -> restoration

=== weeping ===
I wept outside the temple walls. Not silent tears. Bitter weeping. The kind that comes when you realize the magnitude of what you have done — denied the living God, denied your own Master, denied the Son who knew your name before you drew breath. The kind that only the forgiven can produce.
* [The weeping was cleansing.] -> cleansing_tears
* [What came after.] -> restoration

=== cleansing_tears ===
Cleansing tears. Not the tears of someone trying to feel better about a mistake. These were the tears of a soul who had tasted grace and then spat upon it. The kind of crying that strips away all pretense and leaves only naked repentance.
* [Naked repentance.] -> restoration

=== restoration ===
After eight days of silence, He appeared on the shore. Three times He asked me: "Do you love Me?" Three times He said: "Feed My lambs. Tend My sheep. Feed My sheep." The man who had denied Him three times was restored three times — not for what he could do, but for what He had done.
* [The restoration was triumphant.] -> feeding
* [The question was intimate.] -> intimate_question

=== intimate_question ===
"Do you love Me?" — agape. The love that gives itself completely. Not the affection of a casual acquaintance. The love that lays down its life. I had used that word when I promised never to deny Him. Now He was offering me the chance to give it again — with everything I had learned about failure.
* [The love that remains.] -> feeding
* [The grace to try again.] -> closing

=== feeding ===
"Feed My sheep." Not "try again." Not "be more careful." "Feed My sheep." The same charge He gave to all of us on the mountain. The restoration was not about my competence — it was about His commission. The man who denied Him from fear became His voice to the world.
* [The commission endures.] -> closing
* [The sheep would hear.] -> sheep_hear

=== sheep_hear ===
They heard. And they came. The Gospel reached Jerusalem. The Gospel reached Antioch. The Gospel reached the ends of the earth. All from the words of a man who wept bitterly and was welcomed back by the One whose rooster's crow had awakened him to grace.
* [The bitter tears bore fruit.] -> closing

=== closing ===
I am Peter. I denied Him. He restored me. And every day since, I have remembered: the same grace that forgave my denial now flows through me to others. The rooster may crow again, but the Shepherd's love is louder than any bird's call.


-> DONE
